#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kgate: run a heavy gate on the homelab Kubernetes cluster instead of this Mac. Full doc: docs/kgate.md.

    kgate [--profile FILE] [--wait SECONDS] [--cpu N] [--mem SIZE] [--mem-limit SIZE] [--image REF] [--node NODE] [--sched-wait SECONDS] -- CMD [ARGS...]

heavy-gate's command line (`--wait SECONDS -- CMD`) plus the profile and a few overrides. Run it under
`run-and-wake -- kgate -- CMD` and the pane is woken when the job ends.

    kgate cancel [--wait SECONDS] JOB_ID      stop that one job (kgate's id or the run-and-wake id; exact match only)
    kgate cancel --list                       the jobs that are running now
    kgate kc2-setup                           write ~/.kube/kgate-cluster (once, for profiles with cluster = "kc2")

`cancel` is the only supported way to stop a job: it signals that job's one client and nothing else. Never pkill.

Contract
- Ships the git worktree of $PWD (tracked and untracked, non-ignored files, its .git file and the shared git
  folder) to one pod, unpacked at the same absolute paths, and runs CMD in the same $PWD. Ignored files stay here.
  (On KC2 no .git is shipped: the pod builds a one-commit repo from the files, and the Mac's HEAD is in $KGATE_HEAD.)
- Tree hash: the git tree hash of the shipped files is computed here and again in the pod after unpacking. A
  difference fails the job before CMD runs.
- Profile (kgate.toml at the worktree root, or --profile FILE): image (pinned by digest), cpu, memory (the request),
  memory_limit (optional: the limit, default equal to memory), the exact tool
  versions the gate needs, 1Password references for its secrets. The pod checks the versions before CMD; a mismatch
  fails the job with the tool, expected and found.
- Exit status: CMD's own exit code when CMD ran to its end. 64 for a bad command line. 69 when the job could not
  start or finish on the cluster: unreachable, bad or missing profile, secret unreadable, pod never started, tree hash
  differs, tool version differs, the connection to a started pod was lost. 75 when the pod was still Unschedulable (PodScheduled=False)
  after --sched-wait SECONDS (default 600, env KGATE_SCHED_WAIT; homelab only): the pod is deleted and CMD never ran. kgate NEVER runs CMD on this Mac and never
  reruns a job that started. A CMD that itself exits 69 looks the same by code; the record's `reason` tells them apart.
- 1Password reads (profile secrets and the pull token) use the service-account token only: OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN, else
  ~/.config/agent-harness/op-service-token. With neither, the job exits 69 before `op` runs; `op` never reaches the desktop app.
- Files CMD writes to /out in the pod come back to <state>/<id>.out/ before the pod is deleted.
- One JSON line per job in ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/kgate/log.jsonl (keyed by the run-and-wake job id when
  there is one); the full output is in <id>.log beside it.

KC2 (cluster = "kc2" in the profile): the same contract on the Axon KC2 cluster2 cluster, namespace jobs, through local
kubectl and ~/.kube/kgate-cluster (`kgate kc2-setup` writes that file once). No Secrets, no python3 in the pod: the pod side is a
POSIX sh script that prints the same markers. See "KC2" in docs/kgate.md.

Env: KGATE_SSH (default: the one line in ~/.config/kgate/host, rendered from the chezmoi data key sshHosts.kgate), KGATE_SSH_KEY (default ~/.ssh/homelab).
The same file runs in the pod as `kgate.py --pod JOB.json`: it verifies the tree hash, checks the tool versions,
runs CMD and reports peak memory. Needs python3 3.11+ in the image.
"""
